Month = {JAN 23},
Abstract = {In two experiments we examined conceptual priming within and across
   sense modalities by varying the modality (picture and environmental
   sounds) and the category congruency of prime-target pairs. Both
   experiments used a repetition priming paradigm, but Experiment 1 studied
   priming effects with a task that required a superordinate categorization
   response (man-made or natural), while Experiment 2 used a lower level
   category response (musical instruments or animal): one that was more
   closely associated with the basic level of the semantic network. Results
   from Experiment 1 showed a strong effect of target modality and two
   distinct patterns of conceptual priming effects with picture and
   environmental sound targets. However, no priming advantage was found
   when congruent and incongruent primes were compared. Results from
   Experiment 2, found congruency effects that were specific to
   environmental sound targets when preceded by picture primes. The
   findings provide support for the intermodal event file and multisensory
   framework, and suggest that auditory and visual features about a single
   item in a conceptual category may be more tightly connected than two
   different items from the same category.},
