Abstract = {This paper suggests that new directions in mental health and psychiatry
   are being driven by anomalies within epidemiology, mechanisms, and
   integration-based cognition. Three contrasting kinds of new work on
   mental disorder are critically discussed: enactive, ecological and
   active inference approaches, drawing from work by Varela, Gibson and
   Friston. These are detailed together with debates about their
   similarities and differences. All of these positions in fact make common
   use of `affordance' as a core perception-action mechanism, suggesting
   promising options for new or enhanced psychiatric interventions. This
   leads to a critical review of the current state of knowledge on
   affordances, and their limitations, together with some suggestions as to
   how this approach might be applied in mental health and psychiatric
   practice.},
